ded to fill the screen, HD Humanoid rigs dressed for West Baltimore, textured TileMap rowhouses and containers, canvas lighting from street lamps and fire drums, rain at night\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"animation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The engine's procedural skeletal rigs (E.Humanoid) driven by velocity and poses, live dialog portraits, E.Mover movement\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"audio\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Code-written chip music (seven original songs, stealth layers that add drums when you are watched), code-written sound effects (shutter, pager, phone ring, tape), Animalese-style gibberish voices pitched per character and per vowel\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"online\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode: each player runs the corner chapter, sends their detective's position through the hub, sees the others as ghosts, reports progress and a score\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"testing\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Headless Chromium (Playwright) screenshots and scripted playthroughs of every scene with state checks; the AI Hub harness (ghost, 2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter and loss; host-leave), all passing; emulated iPhone 13 (WebKit) and Pixel 7 (Chromium) for the touch-only notice and attract mode. Real iOS Safari and a real controller were not available\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"research\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"None online; built from knowledge of the show and the engine's own documentation\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"environment\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"model\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"usage\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"unknown\\\\\\\"\\\\n },\\\\n \\\\\\\"testingFeedback\\\\\\\": [\\\\n  \\\\\\\"12 issues: the camera not following during the first briefing, center lines drawn as yellow squares, unreadable heat badges, a missing middle-dot glyph, the case board overflowing a 240-pixel screen, the minimize prompt naming Escape (which pauses), a crash when pinning a photo, fiends stuck behind buildings, the player car missing its body at the pier, the heat panel under the touch menu button, the wall clock over the room title, and a syntax error from a bad text edit.\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"3 were game breaking: the crash when pinning a photo (chapter 3 could not be finished), fiends stuck on buildings (the corner could stall), and the syntax error (a blank page).\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Scripted playthroughs that pressed through dialogs like a player and read the game state after each step; they found the board crash and the stuck fiends.\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Waiting in real time for timed events in the browser; reading the state handle told me the same thing faster.\\\\\\\"\\\\n ],\\\\n \\\\\\\"engineFeedback\\\\\\\": [\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Sight, suspicion and sight cones (E.sees, E.Suspicion, r.sightCone) work together out of the box, and dialogs with live portraits made the cast feel real for free.\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"I guessed that a Mover takes world directions from an intent object (it does) and that the pixel font had PlayStation symbols (it does not, so I drew the glyphs myself). I also guessed wrong that E.Dialog's voice is fixed per box; it is read per letter, which let me build the gibberish voices.\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"The camera lens and photo-evidence system: a reticle on the ground, framing, range and line-of-sight checks for each moment worth a picture, and polaroids made from rig portraits.\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Finding which options wall types and props accept (windows, doors, halos, cones) without reading the source: the options are spread across the card, the index and the guide.\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"The overlapping documentation (card, index, guide, kit headers); one searchable reference with an example per option would be enough.\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Add PlayStation and Xbox button glyphs to the pixel font, so input.label() can be printed directly in prompts.\\\\\\\"\\\\n ],\\\\n \\\\\\\"promptFeedback\\\\\\\": [\\\\n  \\\\\\\"The freedom to choose the genre: The Wire is not an action show, and a surveillance game fits it far better than a shooter would.\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"The file name pattern [GameTitle-SOURCE-...] leaves word separators open, and multiplayer 'for the game on its own' could mean local or online play.\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Fitting every requirement (attract mode, hub, controller-first UI, glyph families, touch notice, four chapters) into one session while keeping the tone right.\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"A real DualSense on a TV at ten feet, to judge prompt size and readability where it matters.\\\\\\\"\\\\n ],\\\\n \\\\\\\"aiHub\\\\\\\": {\\\\n  \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"WIRE ME UP\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"tagline\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"a surveillance story of West Baltimore\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Sit on a West Baltimore corner with a long lens, photograph the hand-to-hands without getting made, crack the crew's pager code, work the wire by the rules, and build the case up to a man nobody has ever photographed.\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Game\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"genre\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Stealth\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The Wire\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"series\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"players\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"1–2\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"controls\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"WASD or stick to move, right mouse or L2 to raise the camera, click or R2 to photograph, C or Circle to crouch, E or Cross to interact\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"tags\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"stealth\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"story\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"top-down\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"pixel art\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"co-op\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"crime\\\\\\\"]\\\\n }\\\\n}\\\\njson.dump(d, open('exit-interview.json','w'), indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)\\\\njson.load(open('exit-interview.json')); print('json ok')\\\\nEOF\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Check for stubs and write exit-interview.json\\\"}}}\",\" {\\\"type\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\"